  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nBy Wednesday, a quasi-omega block is shown to develop with an \nanticyclone developing near/over the SoCal coast. Forecast \nconfidence is good that this pattern will hold through the end of \nthe workweek. This will result in temperatures remaining fairly \nsteady across south-central AZ with afternoon highs in the 95-100 \ndegree range across the lower deserts and ranging from the mid 80s \nto lower 90s across the higher terrain areas. Meanwhile temperatures \nare expected to heat up across southeast CA and southwest AZ with a \nstrengthening of the nearby anticyclone. Latest NBM forecast \ncontinues to show afternoon highs reaching the 100-106 degree range \nacross the lower deserts in SE CA and SW AZ for both Wednesday and \nThursday. These temperatures will be over 10 degrees above normal \nand near daily records in both El Centro, CA and Yuma, AZ both days. \nThese temperatures also will likely lead to Major HeatRisk for this \ntime of year, with probability of Major HeatRisk at 50-70%. As a \nresult, an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued for the Yuma area and \nImperial Valley for Wednesday and Thursday. With high pressure in \nplace dry conditions are expected to persist with no chances for \nrain, except for near the White Mtns Wednesday with a weak shortwave \nmoving through the Four Corners.\n\nThe high pressure to the west is then expected to start to weaken by \nthe end of the workweek and into the weekend with a trough moving \ninto western CONUS this upcoming weekend. However, forecast \nconfidence decreases considerably by the end of the weekend and \nstart of the following week as the ensemble spread is large with the \ntiming of the high weakening and moving out of the region and the \ntrough moving in. The ensembles also disagree on the strength of the \ntrough for this upcoming weekend. Either way temperatures are \nexpected to gradually decrease back toward normal by this upcoming \nweekend. Additionally, dry conditions look to continue through at \nleast Saturday, but this could change depending on the evolution of \ntroughing during the weekend. Right now better chances for rain look \nto arrive by the beginning of next week, with the best chances \nacross the higher terrain in eastern AZ. Stay tuned for updates.",
